from science_completion import q,p,m
QUESTIONS=[
q(1,'What is the independent variable?','The factor changed or selected by the investigator.','WS2.2'),
q(1,'What is the dependent variable?','The quantity measured.','WS2.2'),
q(1,'What does accuracy mean?','Closeness to the true value.','WS3.7'),
q(1,'What does repeatability mean?','Similar results from repeats by the same investigator under the same conditions.','WS3.7'),
q(1,'Which reagent tests for starch?','Iodine solution.','RPA4'),
q(1,'What is a positive Biuret result?','Lilac or purple.','RPA4'),
q(1,'At what temperature are school agar cultures normally incubated?','About 25°C.','RPA2'),
q(1,'Which buffer-controlled variable is changed in the amylase practical?','pH.','RPA5'),
q(1,'Name the acidic products causing pH to fall in the lipase milk experiment.','Fatty acids.','RPA10'),
q(1,'What type of sampling uses quadrats at regular intervals along a line?','Systematic sampling.','RPA9'),
q(2,'Why keep temperature constant when investigating amylase activity at different pH values?','- Temperature also affects **enzyme activity**.\n- Keeping it constant helps attribute differences to **pH**, rather than temperature.','RPA5;WS2.2'),
q(2,'Explain why repeating measurements reduces random error but not a systematic zero error.','- Repeats and a mean reduce the effect of **unpredictable scatter**.\n- A consistent zero offset affects every reading, so it must be corrected or recalibrated.','WS3.7'),
q(2,'Distinguish precision from accuracy.','- Precision means repeated measurements **cluster closely**.\n- Accuracy means closeness to the **true value**; clustered readings can all be biased.','WS3.7'),
q(2,'Why should an unusual result not automatically be discarded?','- It may be a genuine biological difference rather than an error.\n- Check the method and repeat; exclude only with a **justified cause**.','WS3.7'),
q(2,'Give two features of a good biological drawing.','- Clear single outlines in proportion, with no invented structures.\n- Accurate labels with straight non-crossing lines, plus a suitable scale or magnification.','RPA1'),
q(2,'Why incubate agar plates inverted and below body temperature?','- Inversion reduces condensation dripping onto and spreading the culture.\n- About 25°C reduces the risk of growing harmful human pathogens compared with body temperature.','RPA2'),
q(2,'Why include a solvent-only disc in an inhibition-zone investigation?','- It checks whether the solvent or disc itself affects growth.\n- The comparison helps identify an effect of the **active agent**.','RPA2'),
q(2,'Why blot plant tissue before final weighing?','- Surface solution would add mass unrelated to osmosis.\n- Consistent gentle blotting removes it without squeezing water from the tissue.','RPA3'),
q(2,'Why use percentage rather than absolute mass change for potato pieces?','- Pieces may have different initial masses.\n- Percentage change relates the gain/loss to starting mass, giving a fairer comparison.','RPA3'),
q(2,'Why sample onto fresh iodine wells rather than adding iodine into the amylase reaction?','- Fresh wells test successive samples without changing the main reaction mixture.\n- Adding iodine to the reaction could alter conditions and the result.','RPA5'),
q(2,'Give one limitation of bubble counting and a specific improvement.','- Bubbles differ in volume, so equal counts need not mean equal oxygen production.\n- Collect **oxygen volume per unit time** instead.','RPA6'),
q(2,'Why allow pondweed to equilibrate after moving the lamp?','- Photosynthesis needs time to respond to the new conditions.\n- Measuring after equilibration makes rates more comparable at each setting.','RPA6'),
q(2,'Why use an unpredictable ruler release in a reaction-time test?','- It reduces **anticipation** of the release.\n- The measurement then better reflects response to seeing the fall.','RPA7'),
q(2,'Why investigate gravity responses in darkness?','- Light can produce a separate directional growth response.\n- Darkness reduces this competing influence, helping isolate gravity.','RPA8','2022 2H Q09.1–09.3: related control reasoning'),
q(2,'Why measure an abiotic factor at each transect position?','- It links abundance to the actual conditions experienced at that site.\n- Assuming distance alone represents light or moisture could misidentify the cause.','RPA9'),
q(2,'Why is a milk-decay endpoint comparison valid only with the same starting conditions and endpoint?','- Different starting pH or endpoints require different amounts of fatty acid.\n- Time differences could then reflect the amount of change required rather than enzyme activity.','RPA10'),
q(3,'Describe a positive reducing-sugar test and its main safety precaution.','- Add **Benedict’s solution** to the sample.\n- Warm in a **hot water bath**, looking for a green/yellow/orange/brick-red precipitate.\n- Use eye protection and handle hot tubes safely, pointing them away from people.','RPA4'),
q(3,'Describe a lipid test using ethanol.','- Mix the sample with ethanol so lipid can dissolve.\n- Add water; a **milky emulsion** indicates lipid.\n- Keep ethanol away from flames because it is flammable.','RPA4'),
q(3,'A plant-tissue sample increases from 2.00 g to 2.30 g. Calculate percentage change and explain it.','- Change = 2.30 −2.00 = **0.30 g**.\n- Percentage = 0.30/2.00 ×100 = **15% increase**.\n- Net water entered tissue by **osmosis** under these conditions.','RPA3'),
q(3,'An inhibition-zone diameter is 12 mm. Calculate its circular area, including the disc.','- Radius = 12/2 = **6 mm**.\n- Area = π ×6².\n- Area ≈ **113 mm²**, to three significant figures.','RPA2;MS5c'),
q(3,'Explain how to determine a reaction-rate proxy from amylase endpoint times.','- Use the time for the **same amount of starch** to disappear.\n- Calculate **1/time**, with s⁻¹ if time is seconds.\n- A shorter endpoint time gives a larger rate proxy.','RPA5'),
q(3,'Explain why a lamp-distance experiment can be confounded by heating.','- Moving the lamp can change water temperature as well as light intensity.\n- Temperature affects photosynthesis through enzyme activity.\n- Use a monitored bath/heat shield and keep temperature comparable.','RPA6'),
q(3,'Three endpoint times are 50, 54 and 52 s. Calculate mean time, range and reciprocal mean time.','- Mean = (50+54+52)/3 = **52 s**.\n- Range = 54 −50 = **4 s**.\n- Reciprocal mean = 1/52 ≈ **0.0192 s⁻¹**; keep the rate-comparison method consistent.','WS3.3;RPA10'),
q(3,'Explain how peer review and replication strengthen a biological claim.','- Peer reviewers assess whether methods, analysis and conclusions are justified.\n- Independent replication tests whether the effect can be obtained again.\n- Together they increase confidence, while neither guarantees a claim is correct.','WS1.6'),
q(4,'Describe preparation and observation of an onion-cell slide.','- Place a thin tissue layer on a slide with a suitable stain.\n- Lower a coverslip at an angle to limit air bubbles.\n- Start at low power, locate and focus, then increase magnification with careful fine focus.\n- Draw observed structures clearly, label them and include appropriate scale/magnification.','RPA1'),
q(4,'Explain how to improve a ruler-drop study that always tests the distracting condition second.','- Practice may make later attempts faster; fatigue may make them slower.\n- Balance or randomise condition order across participants.\n- Keep ruler position, hand and instructions constant and repeat both conditions.\n- Compare mean times and spread using consenting participants and a safe distraction.','RPA7'),
q(4,'Plan a controlled comparison of seedling response to one-sided light.','- Use several seedlings of the same species, age and similar size.\n- Compare one-sided with appropriate all-round light over the same time.\n- Keep water, temperature and growing medium consistent.\n- Record lengths and accurate labelled drawings of root/shoot directions, then repeat and compare.','RPA8','2022 2H Q09.1–09.3'),
q(4,'A study finds taller plants where light is brighter. Explain what can and cannot be concluded.','- The data show a correlation in the sampled conditions.\n- Light could affect photosynthesis and growth.\n- Soil moisture, minerals, species or plant age could also differ.\n- A controlled investigation and broader sampling are needed to isolate causes; the correlation alone does not prove light caused every difference.','WS3.5;WS3.6'),
q(6,'Plan a valid agar-plate comparison of two antiseptics.','- Inoculate sterile agar consistently using aseptic technique and a suitable harmless culture.\n- Apply equal volumes/concentrations to equal-sized spaced discs, adding a solvent-only control.\n- Keep agar depth, bacterial starting amount, temperature and incubation time the same.\n- Tape lids at a few points, incubate inverted at about 25°C and keep cultures closed afterwards.\n- Repeat plates and measure comparable inhibition-zone diameters/areas.\n- Compare results and variation, recognising diffusion can affect zone size; arrange approved disposal.','RPA2'),
q(6,'Plan an investigation of solution concentration and potato mass change.','- Prepare equal-dimension pieces from comparable tissue and measure initial masses.\n- Use a suitable range of known concentrations, equal solution volumes and a water comparison.\n- Keep time and temperature constant, using several pieces at each concentration.\n- Blot consistently and record final masses.\n- Calculate percentage changes and means; plot against concentration.\n- Estimate the zero-change concentration and evaluate scatter, cutting differences and surface-water errors, using sharp tools safely.','RPA3'),
q(6,'Plan an investigation of pH and amylase activity.','- Prepare a pH range using buffers with equal starch/enzyme volumes and concentrations.\n- Equilibrate solutions in a controlled water bath.\n- Mix and start timing, sampling onto fresh iodine drops every 30 seconds.\n- Record the first sample with no blue-black colour, keeping endpoint rules consistent.\n- Repeat and compare mean times or reciprocal-time rates, with a no-enzyme control.\n- Evaluate timing/sampling precision and use appropriate reagent and hot-water precautions.','RPA5'),
q(6,'Plan an investigation of light intensity and pondweed photosynthesis.','- Place comparable pondweed in a solution supplying consistent carbon dioxide.\n- Use several measured lamp distances or measured intensities.\n- Equilibrate and collect oxygen volume for a fixed interval at each setting.\n- Monitor/control temperature and keep plant length, solution and timing consistent.\n- Repeat and compare mean oxygen production rates, using the inverse-square relationship only where suitable.\n- Evaluate gas leaks, lamp heating and plateau-causing factors; keep electrics dry and avoid overheating.','RPA6')]

